<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Safi, Morocco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Safi is a port city on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-jadida">El Jadida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essaouira">Essaouira</a>. Portugal held it from 1508 to 1541 and built a sea fortress there, the Dar el-Bahar, before the advance of the Saadi Sultanate forced its abandonment. Long known for its pottery, it later became Morocco's main sardine port and a centre of the phosphate industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safi-morocco.jpg" alt="Safi, Morocco" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: X wo nd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Safi_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
